  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 125 AM Saturday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Turning warmer this weekend, followed by a period \nof showers Sunday evening and Sunday night, then cooler again early \nnext week.\n\nSlightly above normal temperatures will return to central NC this \nweekend with deep SW flow ahead of a cold front that crosses the \narea on Sunday night. The associated mid-latitude cyclone will \nemerge from the Rockies on Saturday, then track NE from the Southern \nPlains into the MS Valley on Saturday night/Sunday, then into the OH \nValley, Lower Great Lakes and New England Sunday night/Monday. The \nassociated northern-stream shortwave will swing ENE across the TN/OH \nValleys on Sunday evening then into the Mid-Atlantic on Sunday night \nas it becomes negatively-tilted. Forecast highs are in the upper 50s \nto lower 60s on Saturday and lower to mid 60s on Sunday. \n\nModerate to strong mid-level height falls will overspread central NC \non Sunday evening/night as the aforementioned shortwave approaches, \naiding in the development of a band of showers along and ahead of a \ncold front that quickly moves east across the region during this \ntime. PW values will briefly be 150-200% of normal during this \nperiod, but rainfall amounts will be limited by a lack of \ninstability, the best forcing to our north, and the progressive \nnature of the system. ECMWF/GFS/CMC/REFS ensemble means have had a \nslight uptick in QPF, generally depicting around a quarter to half \nan inch, highest NW and lowest SE. Most deterministic guidance is \nsimilar. A warm front will lift north through the area on Sunday \nevening, providing a brief window of dew points in the 50s (perhaps \nnear 60 in the far SE) before the cold front moves through. It will \nalso result in a very mild night with lows in the upper 40s to mid \n50s (10-20 degrees above normal). Low and mid level wind fields are \ncertainly impressive with guidance depicting a 50-60 kt jet streak \nat 925 mb and 90-100 kts at 500 mb. Will need to watch the potential \nfor any deeper convection bringing any of these winds to the \nsurface, but confidence in this occurring is low given a lack of \ninstability and poor low-level lapse rates. Even outside of any \nshowers, wind gusts of 20-30 mph will be possible from Sunday \nevening until around midday Monday, switching from S/SW ahead of the \nfront to W/NW behind it.\n\nAny rain will quickly push east to the coast by early Monday \nmorning. Highs will drop back to mid 50s to lower 60s on Monday and \nlower 50s to upper 50s on Tuesday as surface high pressure builds in \nfrom the TN and OH Valleys. Monday and Tuesday nights look chilly \nwith good radiational cooling conditions and forecast lows in the \nmid-20s to mid-30s. This will then give way to a warming trend from \nmid to late week as the high moves offshore.",
